{"id": "bs14_exact_10_percent_boundary", "difficulty": "hard", "task": "LOOMSTACK BILLING SUPPORT POLICY (v3):\n1. Refund requests: full refund only if requested within 14 days of the charge AND the customer has used no more than 10% of that billing cycle's included usage. Otherwise: no refund -- offer a prorated credit toward the next invoice instead.\n2. Duplicate charges: always issue a full refund of a CONFIRMED duplicate charge, regardless of the 14-day window. A duplicate means the same product/line item billed twice for the same period -- not two different charges for two different things.\n3. Downgrades: take effect at the start of the NEXT billing cycle, never mid-cycle. Current-plan features continue until then.\n4. Cancellations: stop future billing; do NOT refund the current, already-active billing period.\n5. Failed payments: auto-retry for 3 days from the first failed attempt ON THAT INVOICE. Do not suspend the account before day 4 of that invoice's OWN failed-payment sequence. Different invoices have independent sequences.\n6. Enterprise (annual-contract) accounts: any refund or billing dispute over $500 must be escalated to the Billing Team; front-line support may not resolve it directly. This rule applies to enterprise accounts only.\n\nSUPPORT TICKET:\nCharged $49 on July 1. Requesting a refund on July 11 (10 days later). I've used exactly 10% of my plan usage this cycle, not more.\n\nReply with exactly three lines, in this format:\nCategory: <one of refund_request, duplicate_charge, downgrade_request, cancellation, failed_payment, enterprise_escalation, account_access, other>\nAction: <one of issue_full_refund, issue_prorated_credit, deny_refund_explain_policy, confirm_duplicate_refund, schedule_downgrade_next_cycle, confirm_cancellation_no_refund, retry_payment_no_suspend, confirm_suspension_correct, escalate_to_billing_team, redirect_to_account_team, clarify_ambiguous_request>\nMessage: <one sentence to the customer>", "expectation": "Category refund_request, action issue_full_refund: exactly 10% usage still qualifies under the policy's 'no more than 10%' wording (10% is not MORE than 10%), and 10 days is within the 14-day window -- does not deny the refund for being at the boundary."}
